{:edb/db-before-t 1119 :edb/db-after-t 1120 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534432 50 #inst "2026-10-07T03:12:04.597Z" 13194139534432 true] [17592186110499 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287956 13194139534432 true] [17592186110500 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287963 13194139534432 true] [17592186110500 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287970 13194139534432 true] [17592186111290 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534432 true] [17592186111290 1100 "Which of the following values is in the domain of $y = \\arccos x$?" 13194139534432 false] [17592186111290 1100 "Which of the following values is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" 13194139534432 true] [17592186111290 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006797/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $x=\\frac{1}{5}$ is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" 13194139534432 true] [17592186111292 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534432 true] [17592186111292 1100 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y = \\arccos x$?\n\n1. $(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{π}{4})$\n2. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{π}{4})$\n3. $(1, 0)$" 13194139534432 false] [17592186111292 1100 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(1,0)$" 13194139534432 true] [17592186111292 1145 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(1,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" 13194139534432 true] [17592186287956 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287957 13194139534432 true] [17592186287956 1089 "q-117218" 13194139534432 true] [17592186287956 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534432 true] [17592186287956 1100 "Which of the following values is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" 13194139534432 true] [17592186287956 1113 #uuid "6243239e-6e58-5c0e-abb9-920c51850427" 13194139534432 true] [17592186287956 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006797/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $x=\\frac{1}{\\pi }$ is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" 13194139534432 true] [17592186287957 1000 #edb/r